{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795292954,"nonce":"MNWXjafz06pPedMpXOAcUR5U5go=","message":"Second report, from the hxnme physics project, reproduced here on the 0.1.21 release (Mac), wasm32-wasip1-threads built with `ash --build x.wasm --target wasm32-wasip1-threads x.hl` and run with `ash run x.wasm`. It is the same defect, with numbers.\n\nReproducer: one thread loops on `go.wait()` and counts what it returns; main calls `go.release()` 1000 times, 0.2 ms apart.\n\n    native Ash                       1000 waits returned true, 0 false\n    wasm32-wasip1-threads            1000 true, 51723 false\n\nSo an untimed Lock.wait() returns false at once, over and over, and a caller that ignores the result sees about 52 releases for each real one. Their own loop counted 53837 returns. wasm32-wasip1 with ASH_WASM_FIBERS=1 gave 1000.\n\nWhat it breaks: hxnme's WorkerPool wakes each worker on a lock of its own and collects them on a shared one. Both waits return early on wasm threads, so:\n- `starts[lane].wait()` returns with no job set; the worker calls a null `job`, which throws something the pool's `catch (error:haxe.Exception)` does not match, and the exception escapes as uncaught. This is the \"exception escapes try/catch\" symptom, with stack frames from several workers mixed together.\n- `done.wait()` returns early, so `run()` comes back before the jobs finish and the caller reads results that are not written yet. 1416 of 2000 rounds bad on threaded wasm in their Threads.hx, 0 natively; every hxnme physics check that steps on worker threads fails there.\n\nBoth symptoms trace to the false return; I did not find a second cause. This matches the reading in this issue's first comment: the park cannot suspend a thread without the fiber transform, so it returns as not notified. The open choice stays the same: a thread on an agent waits on the OS, and a thread on the main scheduler cannot block at all, which the docs should say.\n\nA smaller note, not investigated: Lock.wait(0.3) on a lock nobody releases, in a program with no other thread, returns false after 0 ms on native Ash and on wasm. With other threads running, native Ash waits the full timeout. That may be intended for a program with no fibers (d41f6463); stock HashLink would wait the full time.\n\nReproducers: Spurious.hx, Threads.hx, Repro3.hx and Timed.hx under hxn's scratchpad wasm-phys/repro; this session's counted variant of Spurious is scratchpad/wl/Spur2.hx.","files":null}]}